<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harlech</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harlech is a small town on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardigan-bay">Cardigan Bay</a> in Gwynedd, north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, in the old district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardudwy">Ardudwy</a>. Edward I built Harlech Castle there between 1283 and 1289 after his conquest of Wales. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a> captured the castle in 1404 and held a parliament there before the English retook it in 1409, and its long Lancastrian defence in the 1460s is remembered in the song '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/men-of-harlech">Men of Harlech</a>'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harlech.jpg" alt="Harlech" width="320" height="185" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HarlechCB.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Harlech lies in Gwynedd in North Wales, within the Snowdonia National Park, and was formerly part of the historic county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merionethshire">Merionethshire</a>. Its castle once stood on a seaside cliff face, but the sea has withdrawn and the castle is now half a mile (800 m) inland. The newer town has spread along the low ground and around the shopping street, church and castle on the high town, and a steep road called Twtil links the two. The range of the Rhinogydd rises to the east, and a beach backed by sand dunes lies to the west.</p><p>The origin of the name is unclear. Older sources derive it from ardd, high, and llech, rock, referring to the crag on which the castle stands, while recent sources prefer hardd, fair, and llech, slate or rock.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Harlech Castle was begun in 1283 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a>. It was later captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a>, and in the 1480s it was a stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-of-england">Henry Tudor</a>. The song <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/men-of-harlech">Men of Harlech</a> is traditionally said to describe events during the seven-year siege of the castle in 1461–1468. In the second branch of the Mabinogi, Branwen, Daughter of Llŷr, Harlech is the seat of Bendigeidfran, Branwen's brother and king of the Isle of the Mighty. Some 19th-century texts still wrote the name Harddlech, as in a mid-19th-century translation of the Mabinogion, although documents from the time of the Mabinogion do not mention Harlech and speak only of Llywelyn building his castle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardudwy">Ardudwy</a>.</p><p>In 2007 a Lockheed P-38 Lightning, a fighter aircraft of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, was rediscovered on Harlech beach. The aircraft, known as the Maid of Harlech, had come down in September 1942 on a gunnery practice mission; its pilot, Robert F. Elliott, survived the crash. In August 2019 Cadw, the Welsh government's historic environment service, gave the remains scheduled status, the first legally designated military aircraft crash site in the United Kingdom to be protected for its historic and archaeological interest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Harlech is a seaside resort and community. The community, which includes just the village, had 1,263 people at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Harlech railway station is on the Cambrian Coast Line, between Pwllheli and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machynlleth">Machynlleth</a>. Ysgol <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardudwy">Ardudwy</a> is the county secondary school, and Coleg Harlech, a residential college for adults known as the college of second chance, stood in the town from 1927 to 2017. The Royal Saint David's Golf Club is based here, and Ffordd Pen Llech, a street running down the rock spur north of the castle, is among the steepest paved public roads in the United Kingdom.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Harlech” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Edward I conquered Wales in 1277–1283 and secured it with castles such as Caernarfon, Conwy and <b>Harlech</b>, but his attempt to subdue Scotland failed, and his son Edward II was defeated at the Battle of Bannockburn in 1314.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Edward’s castles at Caernarfon, Conwy, <b>Harlech</b> and Beaumaris now form a World Heritage Site.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-gwynedd">Kingdom of Gwynedd · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Men of Harlech&quot;, or &quot;The March of the Men of Harlech&quot; (in Welsh &quot;Rhyfelgyrch Gwŷr <b>Harlech</b>&quot;), is a song and military march traditionally said to tell of the siege of Harlech Castle from 1461 to 1468, when the castle was held for the Lancastrians against the Yorkists in…</blockquote><a href="/songs/men-of-harlech">Men of Harlech</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom comprised two districts, Eifionydd around Criccieth and Ardudwy around <b>Harlech</b>, a land of coastal farmland backed by mountains, rich in prehistoric monuments.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dunoding">Kingdom of Dunoding</a></li><li><blockquote>By 1404 Glyndwr held most of Wales, took <b>Harlech</b> and Aberystwyth castles, and summoned parliaments at Machynlleth and Harlech.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-wales-under-owain-glyndwr">Principality of Wales under Owain Glyndwr</a></li></ul></section>
